{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT-CAUSED VIA /rebar-debug. Full protocol, isolated worktree, three hypotheses tested at runtime. THE HEADLINE: two of the three mechanisms are INTENDED BEHAVIOUR and I did not touch them.\n\nH1 — the bound firing: INTENDED, not a defect. Ran _validate_recovery_inputs directly: raises at 24,001; does NOT raise at exactly 24,000; raises for c97f's real 25,516 carrying diagnostic {'context_chars': 25516, 'context_char_limit': 24000}. Legitimacy rests on the module docstring ('Reject hostile/unbounded recovery work before the first recovery call'), docs/llm-framework.md:487-508 documenting the exact numbers, and tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py::test_unbounded_ticket_context_fails_before_any_recovery_call which asserts len(runner.requests) == 1 — ZERO billable recovery calls on a breach. Raising or bypassing the bound would have regressed a deliberate cost safeguard. completion_recovery.py is CONFIRMED UNTOUCHED by this change.\n\nH2 — the apparent flakiness: INTENDED, and this was the decisive experiment. With the SAME oversized 25,516-char context, a non-truncating primary returns normally and _validate_recovery_inputs is called ZERO times; with a truncating primary the abort is deterministic and the runner is called exactly once. So entry into recovery is variable (it depends on whether the live primary call hits finish_reason=length), while failure once entered is deterministic. That fully explains 'same command, different outcome' without any nondeterminism in the bound itself.\n\nH3 — the misleading message: THE DEFECT. Confirmed at runtime that outcome_of(CompletionRecoveryError(...)) is None and hasattr(exc,'outcome') is False — .outcome is attached only at runner.py:194 and :494, and this error is raised inside completion_recovery.py, so it never passes those seams. It therefore fell through the single catch-all in transition_close.py to the generic 'install the agents extra' text, while .diagnostic (which already holds the numbers) was discarded.\n\nFIX + THE TRAP I CAUGHT IN IT. The first version of the fix read the diagnostic keys unconditionally, so a recovery error from a DIFFERENT stage rendered 'The ticket's context (None chars) exceeds the bounded completion-recovery limit (None chars)' for an exhaustion at criterion 7 — nonsense AND a false diagnosis, i.e. the same misattribution class simply relocated. I pinned that RED first, then made the size claim conditional on the bound keys actually being present.\nTESTS (4, held out from the fixer): the bound case reports size+limit and asserts no missing-extra; a NEGATIVE CONTROL that a genuine unavailability STILL gets the install-the-extra guidance (so narrowing cannot decay into deleting); the non-bound recovery case must not claim a size breach; and a collateral invariant that a bound breach never exits 11 (docs/exit-codes.md reserves 11 for retryable — an agent auto-retrying a fixed bound would loop).\nMUTATION-CHECKED: re-introducing the exact root cause (neutralising the isinstance branch) returns the test to RED; restored, 4/4 green.\nREGRESSIONS: the protected contract test (12 passed) and the close-gate failure matrix (35 passed) both pass; ruff + mypy clean; no new dependency; no pre-existing test modified; sibling sweep found NO other site emitting that remedy. Three unit-tier errors (build-provenance / engine-dir wheel tests, 'leaked entries into REPO_ROOT') were proven ENVIRONMENTAL by running them with my change stashed — identical failures on pristine source; they are an artifact of my PYTHONPATH harness in a venv-less worktree, not of this fix.\n\nSCOPE HONESTY: this makes the failure ACCURATE, not absent. An oversized ticket still cannot use bounded recovery — by design. Adding a plan-review-style escalation ladder (batch → one-at-a-time → larger window → typed 'reduce the ticket' finding, per plan_review/orchestrator.py:6-15 and det_floor.py:619-656) would be a genuine DESIGN CHANGE, so it is deliberately NOT in this fix; the remaining ACs on this ticket about reconciling the bounds are left open for that decision.\nPushed as change 1027."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785395807202085001,"uuid":"48554a8f-6987-491f-aac7-c0a2f07de5f7"}